<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Exploring My Community – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring My Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Park!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple phrases or sentences about personal experiences and familiar items with support and guidance. Interactive: Contribute a word to a class story or sentence, with help.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple sentences about visiting community places with support (e.g., "I go to the market with my mom."). Can contribute a word or phrase to a class project about community places with support (e.g., adding "park" to a class story about the neighborhood). Can write basic words or simple sentences about community places (e.g., "park," school," and "library").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a quick "What's in our community?" review. Students look at flashcards of community places (park, market, school, library, church) and say the names. The teacher writes the words clearly on the board as students say them, pointing to each word as they say it. This reinforces word recognition for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a 'park' and says, "It's the park. The park is big. I play there." The teacher writes these three simple sentences on the board clearly, pointing to each word as they write it. The teacher then repeats this for 'market', writing "It's the market. The market is busy. I go there." This models simple sentence structure and target vocabulary for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What place is this?" (pointing to 'park' flashcard and the word on the board) -> Expected answer: "Park!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you see the word 'play'?" (pointing to the written sentence "I play there.") -> Expected answer: "Yes!" or pointing to the word.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring My Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated text (e.g., a short, descriptive paragraph or "My Community Book" page) displayed on a projector or large chart paper. The text contains simple sentences about community places:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Hello! My name is Ana. I live in a nice community. I go to the park. The park is big. I play with my friends there. Next to the park is a small market. My mom and I visit the market. We buy fruit. I also go to school. The school is tall. I learn new things there."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Describing places and activities: "The park is big. I play there." Answering questions: "Where is the library?" "Next to the market."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to the words. Students listen and follow along.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What places does Ana talk about?" (Students can point to pictures or say words like "park," "market," "school.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher writes sentence beginnings on the board (e.g., "The park is...", "My mom and I visit the...") and students identify the correct ending from the text orally or by pointing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic awareness:</b> Teacher points to "park" and "market" in the text and asks students to identify the initial sound /p/ and /m/. "What sound do you hear at the start of 'park'?" "What sound do you hear at the start of 'market'?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple (e.g., "It's the market."), Adjectives + nouns (e.g., "big park"), WH-questions (e.g., "Where is it?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> park, market, school, library, church, store, go, visit, play, big, small, next to.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with simple sentences and blanks for community places or activities. Students complete the sentences using word bank cards or by referring to words on the board.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I go to the _______." (park / school / market)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The park is _______." (big / small)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I _______ in the park." (play / run)</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher monitors and provides support with spelling and word choice.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Guided Sentence Writing):</b> The teacher provides picture prompts of community places (e.g., a park, a school, a market). For each picture, students are given a sentence frame to complete in their notebooks or on small whiteboards.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of a park: "This is a _______ park. I _______ there."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of a school: "This is a _______ school. I _______ new things there."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher walks around, checking students' writing, offering immediate feedback on word choice and simple sentence structure.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Word Check):</b> Students work in pairs. They show their completed sentences from the previous activity to their partner. Partner points to the words they can read and checks if the words match the pictures. (e.g., "Park? Yes! Play? Yes!") The focus is on recognizing familiar words and simple understanding, not perfect grammar.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring My Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "My Favorite Community Place" Drawing and Writing activity. Each student receives a blank sheet of paper or a small template. They will draw their favorite community place and write 1-2 simple sentences about it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one community place they like (e.g., park, market, school, home, library).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They draw a picture of their chosen place on their paper.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, they write 1-2 simple sentences describing the place or what they do there, using the target vocabulary and sentence structures modeled (e.g., "It's the park. The park is big. I play there."). The teacher provides a sentence starter on the board for support: "This is my favorite place. It's the _______. I _______ there."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, offering assistance with spelling and sentence formation, encouraging students to use adjectives like "big," "small," "friendly."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sociolinguistic: Teacher encourages students to describe their place respectfully. "Is it a clean park?" "Yes, it is clean."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> To produce simple, legible written sentences about a familiar community place with support, demonstrating use of target vocabulary and basic sentence structure.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for written sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students display their "My Favorite Community Place" drawings and written sentences. A few volunteers can share their work by holding it up and reading their sentences aloud to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., used "park," "market," "school," "go," "play," "big," "small").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Simple sentence structure:</b> (e.g., S-V-O sentences like "I go to the park.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Legibility:</b> Is the writing clear enough to read?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task completion:</b> Did the student draw a place and write at least one sentence?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick drawing or statement.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write about the _______." (Students write the name of one community place they wrote about).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a happy face if you could write 2 sentences, a neutral face for 1 sentence, and a sad face if it was hard.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new word did you write today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or hard to write about your favorite place?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., the sentence frames, visual prompts, peer checking). Students were engaged with drawing and writing about their favorite places.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students still struggle with basic spelling of high-frequency words; need to provide more explicit word banks or individual support. More practice with sentence sequencing for students who could only write single words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their knowledge of community places by creating a class "Community Map" project, which will involve mediating information and presenting their written contributions in a larger context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>